      "phase": null,
      "text": "Can you run that with nODD?",
      "redacted": "no"
    },
    {
      "id": "msg_000b64fbd11f6152016aed14dc236487d28d8b1afb586baea6",
      "role": "assistant",
      "phase": "commentary",
      "text": "I’ll run the electron scan on the tiled nODD endcap and compare it with a continuous-silicon control in the same absorber stack. I’ll keep this to raw Geant4 response, leave the Marlin study deferred, and add the results and plots to PR #118.",
      "redacted": "no"
    },
    {
      "id": "msg_000b64fbd11f6152016aed26b5a79887d295e86ad5e15d815c",
      "role": "assistant",
      "phase": "final_answer",
      "text": "Completed with nODD’s tiled geometry: **7,600 electron events** across 5, 20 and 50 GeV, plus 100 reference events.\n\nAt 20 GeV, compared with an idealized continuous-silicon control:\n\n| Target position | Mean silicon signal reduction |\n|---|---:|\n| Selected tile centre | **9.4% ± 0.4 percentage points** |\n| Selected inter-tile gap | **65.4% ± 0.2 percentage points** |\n\nThese are local raw-signal effects. The control fills gaps and activates guard regions, so this does **not** establish an overall calorimeter energy loss or reconstructed resolution.\n\nThe three plots, data and reproduction recipe are integrated into [PR #118](https://github.com/asalzburger/nodd/pull/118). Local checks and separate agent review passed; hosted CI is pending. Marlin remains deferred.",
      "redacted": "no"
    }
